# Rewards categories (not MCC)

Card reward rules and transaction categorization share one vocabulary in
`data/card_catalog/reward_categories.json`:

  dining | groceries | travel | gas | entertainment | shopping | other

## Flow

```text
Chase CSV Category  →  rewards category  ┐
user's saved answer (authoritative)      ├→  Transactions.category  →  Reward_Rules match
neither  →  ""  →  review queue          ┘
```

`normalize_csv` owns the bank's vocabulary. `resolve_category(row, user_id)`
checks that user's override (Redis, then `MerchantResolution`), then falls back
to the adapter's stamp. There is no Visa MCC hop and no LLM.

## The two states that are easy to confuse

- `"other"` — a real bucket: fees, utilities, card payments, anything no card
  pays a bonus on.
- `""` (`UNRESOLVED_CATEGORY`) — nobody knows yet. Surfaced by `GET /api/review/`.

Never collapse `""` into `"other"`. That is the bug the review queue exists to
prevent: it hides unknowns inside a legitimate-looking category.

## Overrides are per user

`MerchantResolution` is keyed `(user, merchant_key)` and the Redis key is
`merchant:{user_id}:{merchant_key}`. A global override table would let one
person's label rewrite everyone else's categorization.

## Never add a merchant override to...

- dodge an unmapped Chase category (fix `CHASE_CATEGORY_MAP` instead)
- invent precision the bank data does not have

## Before expanding reward_categories.json

Ask the user. New buckets need at least one card rule that uses them and either
an adapter mapping or a review answer that can produce them.
